Roast.pet Frontier Stack =E2=80=94 2 useful finds

1) useknockout/api =E2=80=94 ADOPT/TEST NOW
Source: https://github.com/useknockout/api
Freshly surfaced/updated in current scan. Open-source MIT production
background-removal API based on BiRefNet, explicitly targeting
fur/fine-detail cutouts. ~200ms warm L4 image calls; $0.02/image
hosted; video alpha removal $0.10/output-second; 28 documented
endpoints including masks, PSD export, upscale, inpaint,
collage/studio shots and ProRes 4444 alpha. Self-hostable on Modal;
commercial use permitted. Roast.pet use: replace/augment browser
cleanup when local WebGPU is unavailable or fails QC, and provide a
single backend media-prep API. Smallest test: 50 difficult pet photos
vs Alphaveil/BiRefNet local path, score fur edges, halos, latency and
failure rate. If quality matches, use local-first with Knockout
fallback.

2) Shopify Events payload/subscription update =E2=80=94 ADOPT NOW if Roast.=
pet
Shopify/event automation uses Events API
Official source:
https://shopify.dev/changelog/updates-to-events-payloads-and-subscription-c=
onfiguration
Release: 2026-09-16. fields_changed changed from flat array to {added,
updated, removed}; trigger syntax changes are enforced on next app
deployment; delivery headers also changed. Classic webhooks
unaffected. Relevant to automated order/customer/product flows: old
parsers can misread events after deployment. Smallest test: fixture
tests against new payload shape, update trigger syntax, remove
assumptions about retired headers before next Shopify app deployment.

TOP 5 STACK CHANGES TO TEST NOW
1. Local Alphaveil/BiRefNet primary + useknockout backend fallback.
2. Animal-specific renderer benchmark: LongCat vs H3 Max Lip Sync vs Wan 2.=
7.
3. Multi-take TTS selection before paying for video.
4. Unified audiovisual QC gate before premium rerender.
5. Patch Shopify Events handling before future deployment if Events API is =
used.

CHEAPEST meaningful capability improvement: local browser cutout
remains effectively $0 marginal inference; useknockout gives a very
cheap $0.02/image production fallback.

BIGGEST CAPABILITY SHOCK: no new generative foundation-model shock
this hour. The strongest operational find is a production-grade, MIT,
self-hostable fur-aware cutout API at ~200ms/$0.02 that can remove
custom background-removal backend work.

STACK SIMPLIFICATION: PetPrep interface =3D local browser cutout first
-> confidence/QC -> useknockout fallback -> canonical transparent
PetAsset. This avoids paying cloud inference on easy uploads while
giving difficult fur/low-contrast images a robust backend escape
hatch.

